    {
      "id": "game-description-170657c4-7a80-4198-a540-860c8c2456b9-0",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": "Baseball alternates two teams between batting and fielding. Batters try to reach base and advance around the diamond to score runs; defenders use pitching, catches, and throws to record outs. The absence of a running game clock gives each pitch its own rhythm, with decisions about positioning, stealing bases, and substitutions shaping play over successive innings."
    },
    {
      "id": "game-description-170657c4-7a80-4198-a540-860c8c2456b9-1",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": "Modern baseball developed in the nineteenth-century United States from older bat-and-ball traditions. It has no single securely established inventor. Organized leagues standardized many rules, while informal versions continue to adapt the game to smaller fields, fewer players, and improvised equipment."
    },
